    "applicationMode": "Online through the National Scholarship Portal (NSP). The applicant applies using their One-Time Registration (OTR). The institution must verify the application, and the relevant State Government nodal officer processes verified applications for payment through PFMS/DBT.",
    "officialApplyLink": "NSP : National Scholarship Portal",
    "verificationSourceLink": "https://scholarships.gov.in/public/schemeGuidelines/DEPDGuidelines_1.pdf",
    "applicationWindow": "Check portal for current cycle. The DEPwD guideline requires applications through NSP but does not prescribe fixed annual opening or closing months. Check the live NSP scheme listing/dashboard for the active fresh and renewal deadlines.",
    "lastVerifiedDate": "26 August 2026",
    "researcher": "Manya",
    "notesFlags": "- Department correction: Replace “Department of School Education and Literacy, Ministry of Education” with DEPwD, Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment. - Education-level correction: Do not include PhD in this row. This scheme ends at master’s degree/diploma level. Research degrees are covered by the separate National Fellowship for Persons with Disabilities (NFPwD) component. - There are 17,000 total slots, including renewals. Fresh applications are considered only after renewal cases, so satisfying eligibility does not automatically result in selection. - 50% of available slots are reserved for female candidates. If there are insufficient eligible female candidates, unfilled slots can go to eligible male applicants. This is not a girls-only scheme, so use Disability only as the category tag. - Selection is based on the percentage of disability, not school/college marks. Higher disability percentage receives priority; if there is a tie, the older applicant receives preference. For Specific Learning Disability, cases described only as “40% or above” are treated as 40% for selection ranking. - The ₹1.4 lakh figure is specifically the maximum actual tuition/admission fee reimbursement. It is not a flat ₹1.4 lakh cash grant and does not include the separate maintenance, book, disability and miscellaneous-fee components. - Students may study two courses simultaneously only if the education authority permits it, but this scholarship may be claimed for only one course. - A student who completed one course at a level generally cannot claim the scholarship for another course at the same level in a different subject—for example, B.Com after B.A. However, LLB, B.Ed or B.El.Ed after B.A./B.Sc./BE are specifically allowed if otherwise eligible. - The detailed 2024 guideline permits correspondence/distance/continuing-education courses under this post-matric scheme. Do not mistakenly apply the pre-matric “regular full-time school only” rule to this scheme. - The scholarship may be withheld or cancelled for false information, discontinuing studies, changing the approved subject, unsatisfactory progress, poor attendance or misconduct.",
